      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the autonomic modulation of heart rate (HR) in asthmatic and healthy volunteers to correlate it with the forced expiratory volume in the first second (FEV1)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Ten healthy and 14 asthmatic volunteers were included in this cross-sectional study. The volunteers underwent a cardiopulmonary exercise test, spirometry and a register of both resting heart rate variability (HRV) in the supine and seated positions along with HRV during the respiratory sinus arrhythmia maneuver (M-RSA)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "At rest in supine, asthmatic volunteers presented a higher HR (77.1\u2009±\u20099.9 vs. 68.7\u2009±\u20098.7\u2009bpm), shorter interval between two R waves (R-Ri) (807.5\u2009±\u2009107.2 vs. 887.5\u2009±\u2009112.7\u2009ms) when compared with the healthy volunteers, respectively. Moreover, in the frequency domain of HRV, there was increased low frequency (LF) index (50.4\u2009±\u200917.1 vs. 29.2\u2009±\u200911.1\u2009n.u.) and decreased high frequency (HF) index (49.4\u2009±\u200917.1 vs. 70.7\u2009±\u200911.1\u2009n.u.). During the M-RSA, the asthmatic presented higher HR (82.6\u2009±\u200910.0 vs. 72.4\u2009±\u20097.6\u2009bpm) and lower values of R-Ri (746.4\u2009±\u200992.1 vs. 846.4\u2009±\u200981.4\u2009ms) and approximate entropy (ApEn) (0.7\u2009±\u20090.0 vs. 0.8\u2009±\u20090.1). FEV1 was strongly correlated with the change of the continuous beat-to-beat variability of HR (SD2) index from the seated to the supine position (r\u2009=\u20090.78)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Controlled asthma in adults appears to induce an increased sympathetic modulation and attenuated response to the postural changes and the M-RSA. Furthermore, there is a correlation between the airways' obstruction and HRV, especially during postural changes."
        }
      ]},
